INSERT INTO `soal` VALUES (1,1,1,'<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>A Year Book</strong></p>\r\n<p>A year book is a tradition of high school life in the United States. The book contains the name, picture, and signature of each student in your final year, so that you can remember who you went to school with. Fifteen-year-old New Jersey schoolgirl Catherine Cook and her brother David, 16, wanted more than this. They thought: &lsquo;We don&rsquo;t really know our classmates from just a picture, so why not have a yearbook online? You could learn about people&rsquo;s musical tastes, the movies they like, so on.&rsquo;</p>\r\n<p>Catherine thought of the name (myYearbook.com), the slogan and the logo, with its two smiley faces. To advertise the site, Catherine and David wore T-shirts with the site logo on it to school and soon hundreds of their schoolmates had registered. She spent many evenings talking on the phone to programmers in India, and collecting ideas from her schoolmates. Then her older brother Geoff came up with the money to start up the website.</p>\r\n<p>MyYearbook users can join in the fun by sending instant messages and &lsquo;collecting&rsquo; friends. They can bond with each other by watching the same clips from films and TV programs, and by sharing music, photos, homework and study guides. Some teens feel that they don&rsquo;t fit in at school, but can make friends more easy online. Chatting online helps some shy people not to feel left out.</p>\r\n<p>On the site, members can also earn &lsquo;Lunch Money&rsquo;, a virtual currency, by completing activities on the site, or they can buy it with real cash. They can then spend their Lunch Money on virtual gifts, or donate it to charities in the my Yearbook &lsquo;causes&rsquo; program. Lunch money donations have saved 0,3 km<sup>2</sup>&nbsp; of rainforest, cleaned up 1 million kg of CO2, sent 22.000 books to Africa, an bought 20.000 kg of rice for people without enough food,</p>\r\n<p>Running the website did not stop Catherine and David from completing their schoolwork successfully, and going on to university, where they still managed to put in 50 hours a week developing the site.</p>\r\n<p>Why do US high schools have a year book?</p>',1,'<p>It is because a tradition of high school life in the United States</p>','<p>It is because they want to be able to go to somewhere</p>','<p>It is because they want to be popular</p>','<p>It is because they want to be someone who has high position one day</p>','','C','','','','','','',''),(2,1,2,'<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>A Year Book</strong></p>\r\n<p>A year book is a tradition of high school life in the United States. The book contains the name, picture, and signature of each student in your final year, so that you can remember who you went to school with. Fifteen-year-old New Jersey schoolgirl Catherine Cook and her brother David, 16, wanted more than this. They thought: &lsquo;We don&rsquo;t really know our classmates from just a picture, so why not have a yearbook online? You could learn about people&rsquo;s musical tastes, the movies they like, so on.&rsquo;</p>\r\n<p>Catherine thought of the name (myYearbook.com), the slogan and the logo, with its two smiley faces. To advertise the site, Catherine and David wore T-shirts with the site logo on it to school and soon hundreds of their schoolmates had registered. She spent many evenings talking on the phone to programmers in India, and collecting ideas from her schoolmates. Then her older brother Geoff came up with the money to start up the website.</p>\r\n<p>MyYearbook users can join in the fun by sending instant messages and &lsquo;collecting&rsquo; friends. They can bond with each other by watching the same clips from films and TV programs, and by sharing music, photos, homework and study guides. Some teens feel that they don&rsquo;t fit in at school, but can make friends more easy online. Chatting online helps some shy people not to feel left out.</p>\r\n<p>On the site, members can also earn &lsquo;Lunch Money&rsquo;, a virtual currency, by completing activities on the site, or they can buy it with real cash. They can then spend their Lunch Money on virtual gifts, or donate it to charities in the my Yearbook &lsquo;causes&rsquo; program. Lunch money donations have saved 0,3 km2&nbsp; of rainforest, cleaned up 1 million kg of CO2, sent 22.000 books to Africa, an bought 20.000 kg of rice for people without enough food,</p>\r\n<p>Running the website did not stop Catherine and David from completing their schoolwork successfully, and going on to university, where they still managed to put in 50 hours a week developing the site.</p>\r\n<p>How did Catherine attract her schoolmates to the website?</p>',1,'<p>She wore special pen at school</p>','<p>She wore T-shirts with the site logo on it</p>','<p>She wore trousers with special design at school</p>','<p>She wore tumbler and lunch box with special logo at school</p>','','C','','','','','','',''),(3,1,3,'<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>A Year Book</strong></p>\r\n<p>A year book is a tradition of high school life in the United States. The book contains the name, picture, and signature of each student in your final year, so that you can remember who you went to school with. Fifteen-year-old New Jersey schoolgirl Catherine Cook and her brother David, 16, wanted more than this. They thought: &lsquo;We don&rsquo;t really know our classmates from just a picture, so why not have a yearbook online? You could learn about people&rsquo;s musical tastes, the movies they like, so on.&rsquo;</p>\r\n<p>Catherine thought of the name (myYearbook.com), the slogan and the logo, with its two smiley faces. To advertise the site, Catherine and David wore T-shirts with the site logo on it to school and soon hundreds of their schoolmates had registered. She spent many evenings talking on the phone to programmers in India, and collecting ideas from her schoolmates. Then her older brother Geoff came up with the money to start up the website.</p>\r\n<p>MyYearbook users can join in the fun by sending instant messages and &lsquo;collecting&rsquo; friends. They can bond with each other by watching the same clips from films and TV programs, and by sharing music, photos, homework and study guides. Some teens feel that they don&rsquo;t fit in at school, but can make friends more easy online. Chatting online helps some shy people not to feel left out.</p>\r\n<p>On the site, members can also earn &lsquo;Lunch Money&rsquo;, a virtual currency, by completing activities on the site, or they can buy it with real cash. They can then spend their Lunch Money on virtual gifts, or donate it to charities in the my Yearbook &lsquo;causes&rsquo; program. Lunch money donations have saved 0,3 km2&nbsp; of rainforest, cleaned up 1 million kg of CO2, sent 22.000 books to Africa, an bought 20.000 kg of rice for people without enough food,</p>\r\n<p>Running the website did not stop Catherine and David from completing their schoolwork successfully, and going on to university, where they still managed to put in 50 hours a week developing the site.</p>\r\n<p>Is Lunch Money real or not real, or can it both?</p>',1,'<p>Lunch Money is real because they can donate their money to charities in the my Yearbook &lsquo;causes&rsquo; program</p>','<p>Lunch Money is virtual because the year book is also online</p>','<p>Lunch Money is real and virtual because they can spend it on virtual gifts, or donate it to charities in the my Yearbook &lsquo;causes&rsquo; program</p>','<p>Lunch money is not real and virtual because it is just a program</p>','','B','','','','','','',''),(4,1,4,'<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><strong>A Year Book</strong></p>\r\n<p>A year book is a tradition of high school life in the United States. The book contains the name, picture, and signature of each student in your final year, so that you can remember who you went to school with. Fifteen-year-old New Jersey schoolgirl Catherine Cook and her brother David, 16, wanted more than this. They thought: &lsquo;We don&rsquo;t really know our classmates from just a picture, so why not have a yearbook online? You could learn about people&rsquo;s musical tastes, the movies they like, so on.&rsquo;</p>\r\n<p>Catherine thought of the name (myYearbook.com), the slogan and the logo, with its two smiley faces. To advertise the site, Catherine and David wore T-shirts with the site logo on it to school and soon hundreds of their schoolmates had registered. She spent many evenings talking on the phone to programmers in India, and collecting ideas from her schoolmates. Then her older brother Geoff came up with the money to start up the website.</p>\r\n<p>MyYearbook users can join in the fun by sending instant messages and &lsquo;collecting&rsquo; friends. They can bond with each other by watching the same clips from films and TV programs, and by sharing music, photos, homework and study guides. Some teens feel that they don&rsquo;t fit in at school, but can make friends more easy online. Chatting online helps some shy people not to feel left out.</p>\r\n<p>On the site, members can also earn &lsquo;Lunch Money&rsquo;, a virtual currency, by completing activities on the site, or they can buy it with real cash. They can then spend their Lunch Money on virtual gifts, or donate it to charities in the my Yearbook &lsquo;causes&rsquo; program. Lunch money donations have saved 0,3 km2&nbsp; of rainforest, cleaned up 1 million kg of CO2, sent 22.000 books to Africa, an bought 20.000 kg of rice for people without enough food,</p>\r\n<p>Running the website did not stop Catherine and David from completing their schoolwork successfully, and going on to university, where they still managed to put in 50 hours a week developing the site.</p>\r\n<p>Did the Cooks go to university or work full-time on the website?</p>',1,'<p>Cooks just work full time on the website</p>','<p>Cooks goes to university</p>','<p>Cooks j